# Dependency direction

Dependencies point inward:

- Plugin entry point and commands may depend on application and domain code.
- Application services coordinate domain interfaces and records.
- Domain code must not depend on Paper, Bukkit, JavaPlugin, commands, files, databases, JSON libraries, or instrumentation implementations.

Only `EventLens.java` should extend `JavaPlugin`.

The plugin class is a composition root. It may construct services, register commands, and clean up on disable. It must not contain tracing algorithms or act as a global service locator.

# Suggested logical areas (create when real code exists)

Under the existing base package `dev.bellaouzo.eventlens`:

- `command` — command parsing, permissions, administrator output
- `trace` — session lifecycle and bounded state (partially started)
- `application` — use-case orchestration (started: `StatusQueryService`)
- `domain` — immutable records, limits, sampling, diff policies (started: `EventLensStatus`)
- `paper` — listener discovery, scheduler, lifecycle adapters (planned)
- `snapshot`, `diff`, `filter`, `report`, `observability`, `instrumentation` — planned adapters

Do not create generic `util` or `manager` dumping grounds.

# Paper lifecycle

`onEnable`: validate config, construct graph, register commands, log capabilities accurately.

`onDisable`: stop new sessions, cancel owned tasks, close executors/files, clear references, log one shutdown summary.

Do not use `/reload` as a supported workflow.

# Threading in event paths

Synchronous phase may read supported fields and copy immutable values.

Forbidden on the main thread in hot paths: disk/network I/O, large JSON, blocking on futures, recursive reflection, sleep.

# Observation integrity

Preserve listener order, arguments, thread, cancellation, return behavior, and exceptions.

When preservation cannot be proven, disable the capability and report unsupported.

# Code conventions

Prefer immutable records, constructor injection, final fields, small interfaces, and the plugin logger.

Avoid static mutable state, field injection, broad `catch (Exception)`, `System.out`, and reflection spread across packages.

Add Javadocs to public APIs and non-obvious invariants.
